WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.293 --> 00:00:07.210
PEDRO ARELLANO: Hello,
everyone, and thank you

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.050
for joining me today.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.160
This is Data
Leaders At The Helm.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.140
And in this session,
we're going to take a look

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:17.530
at how technologies and
workplace environments are

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:20.650
evolving, and how these
changes are turning data

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:24.850
leaders into an emerging
force, and are placing them

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:28.120
in a unique position to
really chart a path forward

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.150
for their organizations.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:32.830
My name is Pedro
Arellano, and I'm

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.980
Head of Product Marketing
for Looker at Google Cloud.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:40.400
Those of you
familiar with Looker

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.320
may have noticed that
we have a new logo.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.680
It's very exciting.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.850
We are now part of Google Cloud.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.230
Looker's mission
statement, which

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.040
is to empower people through
the smarter use of data,

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.430
actually aligns very, very
well with Google's mission

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.860
statement, which is all
about organizing the world's

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.350
information and
making it universally

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.580
accessible and useful.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.360
Another thing that Looker and
Google Cloud share in common

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.480
is that we are absolutely
committed to investing

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.808
in multi-cloud.

00:01:09.808 --> 00:01:11.225
And that's a common
theme that you

00:01:11.225 --> 00:01:15.890
would hear throughout
the sessions this week.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.250
Just a bit of quick context
about where the Looker

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.080
product stands today.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.170
Today there are more
than 5,000 developers all

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.810
building on top of
the Looker platform.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.930
We have more than 2,000
companies all over the world

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.260
using Looker, from the
largest retailers and banks,

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.510
all the way down to one
to two-person startups.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.840
And something that will be
pretty central to today's

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.560
talk and other Looker sessions
you attend this week--

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.080
half of our customers
are using Looker

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.175
to work with data in a way that
goes beyond traditional BI.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:52.850
Now, before we get
started, I do want

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.550
to take a moment to
state today's goals.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.550
First, my goal is to--

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.500
hopefully you understand
that the value

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:04.590
of data within an organization
is no longer up for debate.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.040
Everybody needs data,
and everybody's job

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.520
has the potential to
be improved with data.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.560
Second, there are
three trends that we're

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.530
going to identify and
explore in this presentation,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.680
and these trends will
help put into context

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.530
how we've arrived at
this particular moment

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.510
of such great
potential for data.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.630
And finally, we want
you, as data leaders,

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:30.710
to significantly understand
that almost regardless

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:33.410
of your title, you're
now in a position

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.930
to really influence the
direction of your organization

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.430
like never before.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.640
So hopefully this is
why you've decided

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.910
to join this session today,
and it will help you better

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.700
equip yourself with
knowledge and help

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.550
you grow as a data leader
in your organization,

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.660
and also to connect further
with the data community.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:53.800
So let's get started.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.842
Let's get right to the
heart of the presentation.

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:58.770
I'm going to start by
highlighting three trends that

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.110
are contributing to
the success of Looker,

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.810
and trends that we believe
every data professional should

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:08.080
be aware of to maximize
your own potential.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.110
The first trend is that
everybody needs data,

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.940
and that everybody
at an organization

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.870
can benefit from it.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:17.550
In fact, I would argue that more
so than everybody needs data,

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.570
everybody expects data.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:20.680
So why is that?

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.090
Well, let's take a look at
this stat from an IDC research

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.180
paper.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.630
It shows that the
world's data is

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:33.338
expected to reach 175
zettabytes by the year 2025.

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:35.130
And just to put that
figure in perspective,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.330
if each gigabyte in a
zettabyte were a brick,

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.760
we would have enough
bricks to build the Great

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.940
Wall of China 258 times.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.890
And that's just a
staggering figure.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.320
It's a massive
explosion of data.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.240
What exactly is
driving this growth?

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.102
What's driving this
demand for data?

00:03:55.102 --> 00:03:56.560
Well, what's driving
this demand is

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.020
that the data-hungry workforce,
as we call it, has arrived.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.810
Think about it-- today there
are all these job titles that

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:09.310
didn't even exist 10 years ago,
jobs whose entire purpose is

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:13.925
to work with data and deliver
data across an organization.

00:04:13.925 --> 00:04:15.550
And there are so many
roles that simply

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.740
can't function without data--

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:21.370
quantitative marketers,
DevOps engineers,

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:23.620
data-driven product managers.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.470
10 years ago you still needed
to convince your executive team

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.750
of how much data
matters, how important it

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.360
is to be able to push
the organization forward,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.560
and how transformative
it can be.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.590
But the demand for
data is now such

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:39.010
that it's created all of
these job titles around data.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.570
You have digital
marketers who can

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:44.290
be way more effective on
managing their spend using

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:47.410
very innovative applications
like automated bots

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:51.700
that automatically increase
or decrease bids on online ads

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.340
depending on the
performance of those ads.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.950
You have product managers who
can better understand product

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:59.470
usage, and they can
use that understanding

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.327
to know where they
need to invest time,

00:05:01.327 --> 00:05:03.410
and where they need to
invest development efforts,

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:05.230
and also spot opportunities
for expansion.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.630
But despite this growing demand
for data across organizations,

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:15.110
we still find that
66% of companies--

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.720
2/3 of companies-- are
still using spreadsheets

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.750
for the majority
of their analytics,

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.230
which begs the question,
is this the best way?

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.450
Are spreadsheets
really the best way

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.200
to reach people within an
organization using data?

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:31.850
Are the tools that
we're using today

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:35.360
the right ones to reach
this data-driven workforce,

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.250
or this data-hungry workforce?

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.350
And the market has clearly
answered this question,

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.900
and that leads us
to trend number 2.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:47.310
Trend number 2 is that
SaaS business and consumer

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.220
applications are
absolutely exploding.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.760
And yes, this is contributing
to the massive growth in data

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.340
volumes, but it's
also creating new ways

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.920
to reach people with data.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.130
So think about it-- how
many SaaS applications

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.540
have you used just today?

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.240
Just doing a quick
count, I started my day

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.090
on Gmail and Google Calendar.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.970
I used Google Chat
to message my team.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:15.060
I reviewed my presentation
and my notes in Google Docs

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.950
and Google Slides.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:20.415
I've used applications to
look at our customer CRM data.

00:06:20.415 --> 00:06:22.230
I used another
application to understand

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:23.515
our marketing funnel.

00:06:23.515 --> 00:06:25.140
So by the end of the
day, I'll probably

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.500
have used more than
20 SaaS applications.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.020
And what's interesting is that
each of these applications

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:32.700
is actually a data application.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.260
It's generating data.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:35.490
It's crunching data.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:38.332
It's feeding me
data in some way.

00:06:38.332 --> 00:06:41.600
Now, to give you some
context, the Looker team--

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.090
we use more than 40
SaaS applications

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:47.540
to run our business, but
the average enterprise

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.710
uses over 1,000
SaaS applications,

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.585
and that number continues
to grow every year.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.210
Now, as I said, all
these applications

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:58.360
are data applications.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.990
They are data-powered products.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:04.620
It's basically a user experience
built on top of rows of data,

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.180
and their goal is to reduce user
friction when accessing data

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.240
and when accessing insights,
and make the whole experience

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.317
with the data lot
better and a lot easier.

00:07:15.317 --> 00:07:16.900
So I'll give you a
couple of examples.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:19.140
If you're a digital
marketer, your job

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:23.640
is already made better, it's
enhanced, by using Google Ads.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.557
If you're working
with CRM data, you're

00:07:25.557 --> 00:07:26.640
probably using Salesforce.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.670
Salesforce is an enormously
popular CRM system.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.370
And these products are trying to
make data way more accessible,

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.070
to help people in
your organization

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.905
get their job done.

00:07:36.905 --> 00:07:38.280
But it does come
with challenges,

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.620
because these products--
they're very good,

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.610
but they're typically very good
for one particular use case.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.140
They're designed for a
specialized use case.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:48.930
So for example, you
probably wouldn't

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:52.205
use Zendesk to analyze your
advertising performance,

00:07:52.205 --> 00:07:53.580
in the same way
that you wouldn't

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:57.960
use Salesforce to track and
manage your help desk tickets.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.150
So these applications are
already very powerful,

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.850
but that means that
they're also very isolated,

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.210
and you have to jump from
application to application

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:07.350
to really do your job.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:11.160
And what that means is that
you end up missing insights,

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.380
and you end up
missing opportunities

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.120
that are only
uncovered when you're

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.760
able to connect the
data, which allows you

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:18.930
to see the complete picture.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:25.133
So when every department gets
a specialized SaaS application

00:08:25.133 --> 00:08:26.550
like this, it means
that you start

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:30.210
seeing islands of departmental
data start appearing.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.789
The sales team, for example,
will use one set of apps.

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:35.309
They use Salesforce
and a few other apps.

00:08:35.309 --> 00:08:37.059
Marketing will do
the same thing.

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:38.700
They'll use Google Ads.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.679
They'll use Marketo
and something else.

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:43.320
The product team will use
yet another set of apps,

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:44.520
and so on and so forth.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:47.830
Across the organization,
you see this happening.

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:49.860
The problem here is
that each department

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.720
has its own island of data.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.670
They have their
own silo of data.

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.920
But they're all disconnected
from each other.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.110
And they could all
potentially have

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.720
a different, and
even conflicting

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.840
understanding of
critical business metrics

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.790
that should, in theory, be
shared across the organization,

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:08.790
and everybody should have
a common understanding

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.060
about them.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.730
So data professionals--
we refer to this

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:15.860
as an analytics
governance problem,

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:17.420
but it is a very big problem.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:20.050
It's a bigger problem than
that, because it's also a value

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:23.830
problem, because the fundamental
value of business intelligence,

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:27.640
a fundamental belief that we all
share, is that connecting data

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:31.000
makes it way more valuable,
and that it's only

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.340
with connected data
that we get a better

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:36.355
understanding and a more
comprehensive view of how

00:09:36.355 --> 00:09:40.570
a business really functions.

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:43.062
Now, this problem, I'm afraid,
is only going to get worse.

00:09:43.062 --> 00:09:44.520
It's going to get
tougher to solve.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.340
According to IDC, within
the next three years,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:51.670
500 million new business
apps will be created.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:55.170
So if we think that this
is a tough problem now,

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.690
if we think it's hard
to organize data now,

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:59.490
it's only going to get harder.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:02.080
It's going to get
harder a lot faster.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:05.220
So the question
is, what do we do?

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:06.560
Well, something has to change.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.740
According to Forrester,
over 80% of structured data

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:11.992
isn't analyzed.

00:10:11.992 --> 00:10:13.700
And that's really
unfortunate, because it

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:17.390
means that over time, we're
losing valuable insights,

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:21.306
and we're losing the opportunity
to benefit from connected data.

00:10:21.306 --> 00:10:24.480
But fortunately, there is hope.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.790
And that hope is
trend number 3--

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:29.220
data infrastructure
is rapidly evolving.

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.580
So data engineering technologies
have really come a long way.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:37.260
They've changed dramatically
in the last few years,

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.160
and they've really raised the
level of analytics and insights

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.460
that are achievable.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:43.460
If I think back at when
I started my career

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.770
more than 20 years
ago, the technologies

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:49.490
that were considered
the gold standard then

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:53.690
are so, so different
to what we have today.

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.060
The first set of
those technologies

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:59.390
is modern cloud databases--
databases like BigQuery,

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:02.480
like Snowflake, even some
of the AWS offerings.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.040
These are bigger,
faster, cheaper databases

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.970
where you can store
massive amounts of data.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:10.790
You can query the data
at a much lower cost.

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:13.490
You can do it at a much
higher level of performance

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:17.090
than these platforms
of years past.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.020
But these databases are
also getting smarter.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.510
You're seeing things
like augmented analytics,

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:24.240
machine learning, and other
technologies that automatically

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:27.210
generate insights and
are integrated directly

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:29.325
into the database.

00:11:29.325 --> 00:11:30.700
The second thing
you're seeing is

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:33.700
that once you have your data
stored in a modern cloud

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:36.580
database, you can now take
a more modern approach

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:39.610
to integrating and
managing the data.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:42.070
And this modern
approach doesn't depend

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:44.230
on physical data
extracts, and it

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:45.740
can transform data on the fly.

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:48.010
And it's called ELT.

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:52.410
ELT basically takes the
traditional idea of ETL

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:53.620
and flips it on its head.

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:55.170
It makes the
management of the data

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:58.980
ecosystem a lot simpler, a
lot more flexible, a lot more

00:11:58.980 --> 00:11:59.795
agile.

00:11:59.795 --> 00:12:01.670
And you're going to
find, with this approach,

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:03.840
that you'll need
fewer data extracts.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.390
You'll be able to access
data in real time.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.007
You'll be able to access
complete row-level data.

00:12:09.007 --> 00:12:10.590
And your updates--
your data updates--

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:13.960
are going to be
simpler and run faster.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:15.050
But here's the key--

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:17.300
what enables this
new ELT approach,

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:20.120
what makes it possible,
is the growing

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.920
importance of model data.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:26.360
And what I mean by that
is the ability to use

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:31.310
or deploy a semantic model that
describes all of your company's

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:34.460
data, a semantic model that
defines all of your business

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:35.470
logic.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:37.100
And with Looker,
you use a technology

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:40.280
that we call LookML for
your semantic modeling.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:42.110
What's great about
LookML is that it's

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:44.240
a highly collaborative
technology,

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:47.270
and it allows tens or even
hundreds of data engineers

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:50.450
to work together on the
same semantic model.

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:53.930
And what this does is that it
enables real data teams, as

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:57.080
opposed to having
individuals off on their own

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:59.230
doing shadow IT.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:02.380
So it's a very exciting time
to work in this industry.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:04.840
These data engineering
technologies,

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:07.480
or these advancements in data
engineering technologies,

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:10.090
are really changing
our world and our lives

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:12.580
as data professionals,
and they're

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.730
allowing us to bring much
more data to the cloud.

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:17.200
They're making it
faster and easier

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.790
to deploy different
types of data.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:21.970
And as I said, they really
allow your workforce

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:25.120
to work collaboratively
and at scale.

00:13:28.347 --> 00:13:31.100
Now, let me share a glimpse of
how quickly some of these cloud

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:33.140
data technologies
are being adopted.

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:35.750
What you're seeing here is a
view across Looker's customer

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:38.430
base that shows the growth
of different database

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:39.160
technologies.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.080
So you can see some of the
classic data-based technologies

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:46.070
like Postgres and MySQL, and
they're still being used.

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:48.320
But if you pay attention
to the modern platforms,

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.632
like BigQuery and
Snowflake, the growth

00:13:50.632 --> 00:13:52.340
that we're seeing with
those technologies

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:54.870
is just absolutely remarkable.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:57.410
So if part of your motivation
for attending this session

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:59.000
is that your
company's considering

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.610
making a transition or an
investment in a cloud database,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:04.820
now is the time to do that.

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:07.570
Gartner says that within
the next three years,

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:10.820
75% of all databases
in existence

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:12.780
will be hosted in the cloud.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.470
And one of the main reasons
for that phenomenon-- growth

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:18.530
of the cloud-- is the ability
to leverage and manage data

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:20.060
at the scale required today.

00:14:23.372 --> 00:14:24.830
So with those three
trends in mind,

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:27.290
let's wrap up this
section with a description

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:30.880
of what we think success
with data looks like.

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:33.820
The first criteria is that
data needs to be complete,

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:35.650
and it needs to be trustworthy.

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:38.140
There needs to be
confidence in the data.

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:41.290
People need to be able to trust
the insights that your data

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:42.760
platform is delivering.

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:45.460
We talk to companies
all the time who

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:48.130
don't trust the data that
their platform is delivering

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:49.840
because there's no
consistent business

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:53.560
logic across the thousands
of workbooks or spreadsheets

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.030
or reports that are being
used by their employees,

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.270
so what happens is
that instead of having

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:00.770
productive conversations
about the data,

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:03.790
you end up arguing over
whose numbers are correct.

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:06.820
And in order to restore
that trust, what's needed

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:11.350
is a centralized and reusable
semantic modeling layer,

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:13.930
something like what Looker's
LookML technology provides

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:16.970
that allows everyone to work
with the same definition

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:18.170
of key business logic.

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:20.890
So there's never
ever any ambiguity

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:22.420
over what the data means.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:24.540
There is only one
definition of customer.

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:27.290
There is only one
calculation for profit.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:30.000
There is only one definition
of revenue, for example.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.410
And everybody is speaking
the same language.

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:37.100
The second criteria is that
data should be near real-time.

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:39.100
And that's really what
modern cloud databases

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:40.120
allow us to do today.

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:43.510
They make near real-time
insights a reality.

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:47.020
And think of how valuable this
is, really, for any industry.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:49.720
But take retailers, for example.

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:52.750
Imagine if retailers
could optimize

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:55.780
their digital advertising
spend in real time

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:57.250
during the holiday
shopping season.

00:15:57.250 --> 00:16:00.350
It's just massively valuable.

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:02.960
The third criteria is
that data should not just

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:04.830
have an analytical purpose.

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:07.340
It should also have an
operational purpose.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:09.620
And this is a really big
departure from the way

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:13.310
that we've come to think
of data traditionally.

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:16.910
It means that the insights
that your data platform is

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:20.540
generating should have the
potential to trigger actions.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:23.090
And the reason is that modern
business users don't just

00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:24.680
want to understand the data.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:28.190
They want to be able to use
data to get something done,

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:30.380
to take some kind of action.

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.780
What that means
is that we finally

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:36.110
have to actually go beyond
dashboards in the way

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:37.490
that we use data.

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:39.900
Data should feed other systems.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:43.940
It should feed other machines to
help people get real work done,

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:46.070
to help drive the
business forward.

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:50.240
It should not be used just
to display on our report.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:52.730
So let's take a look at a few
examples of how this actually

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:53.900
works in the real world.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:58.960
The first example is a
company called SalesLoft.

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:01.270
SalesLoft is a prospecting
and customer engagement

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:03.280
platform based in Atlanta.

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:05.920
And their Customer Success
Team monitors customer health

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:06.970
scores.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:09.339
And these scores
look at product usage

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:13.270
and alert the Customer Success
Team of any potential issues

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:14.619
early on.

00:17:14.619 --> 00:17:16.240
Now, unfortunately,
this information

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:21.520
has to be manually monitored
and manually looked up in order

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:23.640
to catch low scores
before risking

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:25.569
a customer [? turning. ?]

00:17:25.569 --> 00:17:27.760
What they've done is
now they've replaced

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:30.010
this manual system with
an automated system

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:31.630
that they've built using Looker.

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:35.470
So they combined Looker
with the SalesLoft platform

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:38.110
so that when a customer
health score drops

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:42.130
below a certain threshold, an
alert automatically notifies

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:45.260
the Customer Success
Manager of that problem,

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:48.310
and then automatically
triggers an email

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:50.750
within their engagement
platform to start

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:52.690
reengaging with that customer.

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:56.440
This is massively
valuable for SalesLoft.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:59.190
It increases the relevance of
their customer communications.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:02.410
It increases the impact of
their customer communications.

00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:05.380
And it ultimately creates a
better customer experience.

00:18:05.380 --> 00:18:09.130
And notice how this use case
isn't about showing numbers

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:11.530
on a graph or on a
dashboard, but it's still

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:14.590
an immensely valuable
data-driven application.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:15.820
And just how valuable?

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:20.020
Well, this application helps
SalesLoft preserve $1.2 million

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:22.930
in ARR in the first
four months alone.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:27.810
Another example
is Car Next Door.

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:31.570
Car Next Door is a car-sharing
company in Australia.

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:34.030
The Car Next Door team
was already using Looker.

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:35.580
They were getting
value from Looker

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:37.420
across a number of
different areas--

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:40.690
optimizing marketing, delivering
faster support, more efficient

00:18:40.690 --> 00:18:42.700
product development.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:46.480
But then they used Looker in a
way that was pretty unexpected.

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:49.270
Last year, there were some
pretty severe hailstorms

00:18:49.270 --> 00:18:51.310
that hit parts of Australia.

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:54.310
And these storms cost
really serious damage

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:56.373
to many of Car Next
Door's vehicles.

00:18:56.373 --> 00:18:57.790
And of course, as
you can imagine,

00:18:57.790 --> 00:19:01.630
this resulted in higher repair
costs, insurance, losses

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:05.580
from damaged vehicles that they
had to take out of service.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:07.313
That also covered
potential new risks,

00:19:07.313 --> 00:19:08.980
should something like
this happen again.

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:13.420
So in order to reduce future
downtime and future losses

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:15.040
and to be able to
continue delivering

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:16.560
an excellent
customer experience,

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:19.920
the Car Next Door team quickly
developed a new application

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:23.070
using Looker that would help
them prepare for future weather

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:23.942
events.

00:19:23.942 --> 00:19:25.650
So what they do is
that they use Looker's

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:27.450
geospatial capabilities.

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:29.580
And that allows them
to identify cars

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.940
that are in locations that are
associated with weather events.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:35.070
And in combination with
other technologies,

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:39.450
like Sediment and Braze, they
can send alerts to drivers

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:41.460
to help them get to safety.

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.650
So this is another
example of using data

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:46.105
in a way that does not involve
displaying it on a reporter

00:19:46.105 --> 00:19:49.080
or on a dashboard, but it
does help Car Next Door

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:52.770
identify at-risk cars
and at-risk drivers.

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:54.780
And it improves the
reliability of the service

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:56.730
and it improves the
customer experience, too.

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:58.432
And all of this is
powered by Looker.

00:20:01.090 --> 00:20:04.720
Finally, an example that
anybody in sales will relate to,

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:05.780
this is about Slack.

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:09.790
Slack has a product that they
built called Midas Touch.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:12.580
The problem that the
Midas Touch tool solves

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:16.450
is that their sales reps were
spending many, many hours

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:19.630
every single week creating
customer slide decks.

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:22.060
So they were building
these decks by hand

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:26.050
by literally copying and pasting
content from their BI tool

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:27.880
into Google Slides.

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:30.160
This is very
time-consuming work.

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:31.440
It's very repetitive.

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:33.370
It's very prone to errors.

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:36.530
So of course, it's a perfect
candidate for automation.

00:20:36.530 --> 00:20:40.510
So they envision the Midas
Touch tool-- excuse me--

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:44.860
at its core as integration
between Slack and Salesforce

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:47.600
and Looker and Google Slides.

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:49.300
And what the Midas
Touch tool does

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:53.990
is it allows the sales reps in
Slack to search for customers,

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:56.140
communicating with Salesforce.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:21:00.190
And then it requests a set of
pre-populated, personalized

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:04.630
slides for that customer with
data that's pulled from Looker,

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:06.820
all without leaving Slack.

00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:09.490
So this means that a sales rep
can generate a personalized

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:13.120
report for every single customer
and every single customer

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:15.525
meeting that they go into
with a single command.

00:21:15.525 --> 00:21:16.900
As you can imagine,
it makes them

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:19.648
look like heroes in every
meeting that they go to.

00:21:19.648 --> 00:21:21.190
And it's a really
interesting example

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:22.960
because it's such a
great story of how

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:25.660
data can be used from
multiple different departments

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:27.670
and can be brought
together in order

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:30.070
to massively amplify the
experience for every customer.

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:35.050
Now, these customer
examples show

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:37.570
how the three trends
that we reviewed earlier

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:40.390
are all converging to create
many great opportunities

00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:43.120
for companies to really
build different kinds of data

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:45.370
experiences, and
for data leaders

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:48.910
to influence their companies,
to drive their companies

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:50.827
in this direction.

00:21:50.827 --> 00:21:52.910
Now, when I say data
leaders, what does that mean?

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:53.920
What is a data leader?

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:56.890
Well, data leaders have lots
of different job titles,

00:21:56.890 --> 00:22:00.010
the obvious ones, like Chief
Data Offer, Head of Analytics,

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:03.687
the VP of BI, Data
Analyst, Data Engineer.

00:22:03.687 --> 00:22:05.770
But they can also be people
that don't necessarily

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:10.180
have a Data Analytics title
like a Line of Business Leader.

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:12.070
Essentially,
anybody who can help

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:15.190
shape the data strategy
for their organization.

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:17.620
No matter the title,
these are the people

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:20.650
that are tasked with
creating a data culture.

00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:22.990
And that means
incorporating data

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:26.420
into not just our work lives
but also our personal lives.

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:29.080
So you can see the examples
of these modern businesses

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:30.340
that I shared.

00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:32.890
These data leaders
are the ones who are

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:35.930
driving the company forward.

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:40.030
And that leads us to this idea
that data leadership today

00:22:40.030 --> 00:22:42.468
really is business leadership.

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:47.220
So the key is to
find new use cases

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:50.130
for data, use cases
that fall outside

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:53.170
of the conventional
understanding of BI.

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:55.650
This is a very interesting
quote from Forrester

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:59.100
that talks about how leading
companies are thinking

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:01.920
about data and how
they are driving growth

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:05.850
through differentiating
experiences, products,

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:07.710
and services.

00:23:07.710 --> 00:23:09.720
Experiences, products,
and services--

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:10.720
it's really interesting.

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:13.170
And what I find fascinating
about this quote

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:16.350
is that it talks about the
value that data can provide,

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:20.490
but nowhere does it use terms
like analytics or reporting

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:21.970
or dashboards.

00:23:21.970 --> 00:23:24.870
And the idea is that data
is no longer something that

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:27.930
is used exclusively for
the purpose of analyzing it

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:29.700
on charts and graphs.

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.280
Data is now something
that can be infused

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:34.620
into everyday business
processes that

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:37.410
can fuel operational workflows.

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:39.910
It can be used to build
new products to market.

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:41.910
It can used to make
people smarter,

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:45.510
to optimize your operations,
to create new revenue streams.

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:47.490
And this is very
exciting, because what

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:52.170
we're talking about is expanding
the idea of traditional BI

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:54.840
and really bringing it into
the modern era, the era

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:58.160
of data-driven experiences.

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:00.400
So what can you do
as a data leader

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:03.040
to initiate this change
within your organization

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:05.890
and to bring your
company into this era

00:24:05.890 --> 00:24:08.530
of data-driven experiences?

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:11.950
Well, first of all, think
of modern data technologies.

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:14.260
Think of your current
technology stack.

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:16.410
What are the technologies
that comprise it?

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:17.620
Think of your business users.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:20.950
Do they trust the insights
that are delivered by your data

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:21.880
platforms?

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:24.160
Are these insights real-time?

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:26.770
Understanding the data
technologies landscape

00:24:26.770 --> 00:24:29.480
is obviously fundamental
for data leaders,

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:32.530
but not just to maintain
technical parity,

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:35.350
but also to be able to
innovate and deliver

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:40.090
cutting-edge solutions using
these modern data technologies.

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:41.590
If you're a younger
organization,

00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:43.673
there's a good chance that
you're already digital,

00:24:43.673 --> 00:24:45.100
that you're already
cloud-native.

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:47.710
But if you're a more
established business,

00:24:47.710 --> 00:24:51.760
and maybe it's been a longer
road to migrate to the cloud

00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:53.860
and migrate to
modern technologies,

00:24:53.860 --> 00:24:56.830
you need to start this digital
transformation somewhere.

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:58.190
Where do you start?

00:24:58.190 --> 00:24:59.500
Choose a department.

00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:00.400
Choose a team.

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:03.280
Choose a particular project
to start getting technologies

00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:04.370
and people in place.

00:25:04.370 --> 00:25:09.580
The key really is to find the
right use case and go after it.

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:12.310
Secondly, imagine new
experiences with data.

00:25:12.310 --> 00:25:17.110
As I said before, data is no
longer meant as something just

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:19.060
to be analyzed.

00:25:19.060 --> 00:25:22.690
It's really something
that is meant

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:26.980
to lower the barriers for users,
to fuel operational workflows,

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:30.340
to deliver superior
experiences for your employees,

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:31.690
for your customers.

00:25:31.690 --> 00:25:33.700
The founder of
Looker, Lloyd Tabb,

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:35.500
has this very powerful
guiding principle

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:39.250
that says that great software
is an act of empathy.

00:25:39.250 --> 00:25:41.770
What this means
is it means being

00:25:41.770 --> 00:25:46.390
aware of how users use
technology today, of what tools

00:25:46.390 --> 00:25:47.740
you're using today.

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:50.730
And meeting them with
data in those tools,

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:52.960
it means meeting our
workforce and customers where

00:25:52.960 --> 00:25:57.160
they already work, and bringing
trustworthy data into the tools

00:25:57.160 --> 00:26:00.890
that they already use
and they already trust.

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:02.890
And finally, consider
a new role for you.

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:06.250
Data leaders have an
amazing opportunity today

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:07.690
to really influence
the direction

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:11.260
of their organizations
in very significant ways.

00:26:11.260 --> 00:26:12.790
Every part of an
organization that's

00:26:12.790 --> 00:26:14.860
going through a
digital transformation

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:17.350
is really fueled by
data in some way.

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:21.670
And data professionals who are
capable of not just solving

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:25.460
technology problems, but also
solving business problems

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:28.330
by using data to
create new products,

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:31.780
to discover new value
streams, have become

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:34.070
immensely, immensely valuable.

00:26:34.070 --> 00:26:36.190
And we see this within our
own customer base, too,

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:37.240
all the time.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:41.440
We see data leaders who
have really delivered value

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:43.210
to their organization
and have moved

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:44.980
on to business leadership.

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:47.830
And really, the idea
is that data leaders

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:50.500
are positioned to be the
business leaders of our time.

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:55.125
So what that means,
and to say it

00:26:55.125 --> 00:26:58.770
in a very lighthearted way, is
that the data nerds have won.

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:01.380
Data has been infused
into so many moments

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:02.760
of our daily lives--

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:04.690
at work, at home.

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:08.490
And these data experiences are
brought to us by data leaders.

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:12.240
Yes, engineers and analysts,
but also, as I said,

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:16.118
data-savvy marketing people,
salespeople, product people.

00:27:16.118 --> 00:27:18.660
All of them are data nerds, and
all of them are data leaders.

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:21.820
And as I mentioned, we see
this within our own customers.

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:24.870
We have a lot of examples
of data-savvy individuals

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:28.320
who were able to rapidly
advance their careers because

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:31.260
of the value that they created
for their organizations,

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:35.520
for their customers
through the use of data.

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:38.730
We have customers, folks,
who were data analysts

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:40.270
just a few years ago.

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:42.330
But they became
highly recruitable

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:44.100
and are now in key
business leadership

00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:50.410
positions at their organizations
or other organizations.

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:52.840
So to wrap up, I
hope you recognize

00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:55.660
the enormous opportunity
in front of you today--

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:58.450
both from your company,
but also for your career.

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.760
Data is more valuable than ever.

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:03.340
And people who can find
a way to lead with data

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:05.420
are in high demand.

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:08.060
We're seeing more
demand than ever

00:28:08.060 --> 00:28:11.380
from that data-hungry workforce
and from a customer base.

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:13.090
We see more and more
data that's being

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:16.540
generated by an explosion
of SAS applications.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:18.580
And we continue to see
data infrastructure

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:22.060
evolving and creating new
possibilities for using data

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:26.975
in ways that were just
unimaginable not that long ago.

00:28:26.975 --> 00:28:28.350
So with that, I
want to thank you

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:29.770
for your time and attention.

00:28:29.770 --> 00:28:31.562
And I invite you to
learn more about Looker

00:28:31.562 --> 00:28:34.170
and engage with our team so
that we can discuss your data

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:36.100
and analytics objectives.

00:28:36.100 --> 00:28:37.760
Thank you.

